#import "@local/fgbooks:0.14.0": speech, stanza

== THE NINTH STAGE

And I slept, and dreamed again, and saw the same two pilgrims going down the mountains along the highway towards the city. Now, a little below these mountains, on the left hand, lies the country of Conceit; and from that country there comes into the way in which the pilgrims walked a little crooked lane. Here, therefore, they met with a very brisk lad who came out of that country, and his name was Ignorance. So Christian asked him what parts he came from, and where he was going.

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Sir, I was born in the country that lies off there, a little on the left hand, and I am going to the Celestial City.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But how do you think to get in at the gate, for you may find some difficulty there?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: As other good people do, said he.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But what have you to show at that gate, that the gate should be opened to you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: I know my Lord’s will, and have been a good liver; I pay every man his own; I pray, fast, pay tithes, and give alms, and have left my country for the place where I am going.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But you did not come in at the wicket-gate that is at the head of this way; you came in here through that same crooked lane, and therefore I fear, however you may think of yourself, when the day of reckoning comes, you will have it laid to your charge that you are a thief and a robber, instead of getting admittance into the city.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Gentlemen, you#super[+] are utter strangers to me; I do not know you: be content to follow the religion of your country, and I will follow the religion of mine. I hope all will be well. And as for the gate that you talk of, all the world knows that it is a great way off from our country. I cannot think that any man in all our parts so much as knows the way to it; nor need they care whether they do or not, since we have, as you see, a fine, pleasant, green lane, that comes down from our country, the shortest way into the way.]

When Christian saw that the man was wise in his own conceit, he said to Hopeful in a whisper, “There is more hope for a fool than for him.” Prov 26:12. And he said, moreover, “Even as the fool walks along the way, his sense is lacking, and he shows everyone that he is a fool.” Eccl 10:3. What, shall we talk further with him, or go on ahead of him for now, and so leave him to think about what he has heard already, and then stop again for him afterwards, and see if by degrees we can do him any good? Then said Hopeful,

#quote[“Let Ignorance a little while now muse \
On what is said, and let him not refuse \
Good counsel to embrace, lest he remain \
Still ignorant of what’s the chiefest gain. \
God saith, those that no understanding have, \
(Although he made them,) them he will not save.”]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: He further added, It is not good, I think, to say it all to him at once; let us pass him by, if you will, and talk to him later, even as he is able to bear it.]

So they both went on, and Ignorance came after. Now, when they had passed him a little way, they entered into a very dark lane, where they met a man whom seven devils had bound with seven strong cords, and were carrying back to the door that they saw on the side of the hill. Matt 12:45; Prov 5:22. Now good Christian began to tremble, and so did Hopeful, his companion; yet, as the devils led the man away, Christian looked to see if he knew him; and he thought it might be one Turn-away, who lived in the town of Apostasy. But he did not see his face clearly, for he hung his head like a thief that is found out; but when he had gone past, Hopeful looked after him, and spied on his back a paper with this inscription, “Wanton professing Christian, and damnable apostate.”

Then said Christian to his companion, Now I call to mind what was told me of a thing that happened to a good man around here. The name of the man was Little-Faith; but a good man, and he lived in the town of Sincere. The thing was this. At the entrance to this passage, there comes down from Broadway-gate a lane called Dead-Man’s lane, so called because of the murders that are commonly done there; and this Little-Faith, going on pilgrimage, as we do now, happened to sit down there and sleep. Now there happened at that time to come down the lane from Broadway-gate three sturdy rogues, and their names were Faint-Heart, Mistrust, and Guilt, three brothers; and they, spying Little-Faith where he was, came galloping up with speed. Now the good man had just woken from his sleep, and was getting up to go on his journey. So they all came up to him, and with threatening language ordered him to stand. At this, Little-Faith turned as white as a sheet, and had no power either to fight or to flee. Then said Faint-Heart, Hand over your purse; but as he made no haste to do it (for he was loath to lose his money), Mistrust ran up to him, and thrusting his hand into his pocket, pulled out of it a bag of silver. Then he cried out, Thieves, thieves! With that, Guilt, with a great club that was in his hand, struck Little-Faith on the head, and with that blow felled him flat to the ground, where he lay bleeding as one that would bleed to death. All this while the thieves stood by. But at last, hearing that some were upon the road, and fearing that it might be one Great-Grace, who lives in the town of Good-Confidence, they took to their heels, and left this good man to shift for himself. Now, after a while, Little-Faith came to himself, and getting up, managed to scramble on his way. This was the story.

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: But did they take from him all that he had?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: No; they never ransacked the place where his jewels were; so he still kept those. But, as I was told, the good man was much afflicted by his loss; for the thieves got most of his spending-money. What they did not get, as I said, were jewels; also, he had a little odd money left, but hardly enough to bring him to his journey’s end. Indeed (if I was not misinformed), he was forced to beg as he went, to keep himself alive, for he was not allowed to sell his jewels; but beg and do what he could, he went, as we say, with many a hungry belly most of the rest of the way. 1 Pet 4:18.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: But is it not a wonder they did not get from him his certificate, by which he was to receive his admittance at the Celestial Gate?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: It is a wonder; but they did not get that, though it was not through any good cunning of his that they missed it; for he, being dismayed by their coming upon him, had neither power nor skill to hide anything; so it was more by good providence than by his endeavor that they missed that good thing. 2 Tim 1:12–14; 2 Pet 2:9.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: But it must surely be a comfort to him that they did not get this jewel from him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: It might have been a great comfort to him, had he used it as he should; but those who told me the story said that he made but little use of it all the rest of the way, and that because of the dismay he had at their taking away his money. Indeed, he forgot it a great part of the rest of his journey; and besides, when at any time it came into his mind, and he began to be comforted by it, then fresh thoughts of his loss would come upon him again, and these thoughts would swallow up all.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Alas, poor man, this could not but be a great grief to him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Grief? Yes, a grief indeed! Would it not have been so to any of us, had we been treated as he was, to be robbed and wounded too, and that in a strange place, as he was? It is a wonder he did not die of grief, poor heart. I was told that he scattered almost all the rest of the way with nothing but doleful and bitter complaints; telling, also, all who overtook him, or whom he overtook in the way as he went, where he was robbed, and how; who they were that did it, and what he had lost; how he was wounded, and that he hardly escaped with his life.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: But it is a wonder that his need did not drive him to selling or pawning some of his jewels, that he might have the means to relieve himself on his journey.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: You talk like one upon whose head the shell is to this very day. For what should he pawn them? Or to whom should he sell them? In all that country where he was robbed, his jewels were not valued; nor did he need the relief that could be given him from there. Besides, had his jewels been missing at the gate of the Celestial City, he would have been (and he knew that well enough) excluded from an inheritance there, and that would have been worse to him than the appearance and villainy of ten thousand thieves.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Why are you so tart, my brother? Esau sold his birthright, and that for a mess of pottage, Heb 12:16; and that birthright was his greatest jewel: and if he did, why might not Little-Faith do so too?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Esau did sell his birthright indeed, and so do many besides, and by doing so exclude themselves from the chief blessing, as that wretch also did; but you must put a difference between Esau and Little-Faith, and also between their states. Esau’s birthright was a type; but Little-Faith’s jewels were not so. Esau’s belly was his god; but Little-Faith’s belly was not so. Esau’s need lay in his fleshly appetite; Little-Faith’s did not. Besides, Esau could see no further than the fulfilling of his lusts: For I am about to die, said he, and what good will this birthright do me? Gen 25:32. But Little-Faith, though it was his lot to have only a little faith, was by his little faith kept from such extravagances, and made to see and prize his jewels too much to sell them, as Esau did his birthright. You do not read anywhere that Esau had faith, no, not so much as a little; therefore it is no wonder, where the flesh alone bears sway (as it will in that man where there is no faith to resist it), if he sells his birthright and his soul and all, and that to the devil of hell; for it is with such as it is with the donkey, who in her heat cannot be turned away, Jer 2:24: when their minds are set upon their lusts, they will have them, whatever they cost. But Little-Faith was of another temper; his mind was on things divine; his livelihood was upon things that were spiritual, and from above: therefore, to what end should he who is of such a temper sell his jewels (had there been any who would have bought them) to fill his mind with empty things? Will a man give a penny to fill his belly with hay? Or can you persuade the turtle-dove to live upon carrion, like the crow? Though faithless ones can, for carnal lusts, pawn, or mortgage, or sell what they have, and themselves outright to boot; yet those who have faith, saving faith, though only a little of it, cannot do so. Here, therefore, my brother, is your mistake.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I acknowledge it; but yet your severe reflection had almost made me angry.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, I only compared you to some of the birds that are of the brisker sort, which will run to and fro in untrodden paths with the shell still upon their heads: but pass by that, and consider the matter under debate, and all shall be well between you and me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: But, Christian, these three fellows, I am persuaded in my heart, are only a company of cowards: would they have run otherwise, do you think, as they did, at the noise of one who was coming on the road? Why did not Little-Faith pluck up a greater heart? He might, I think, have stood one brush with them, and yielded when there was no remedy.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: That they are cowards, many have said, but few have found it so in the time of trial. As for a great heart, Little-Faith had none; and I perceive by you, my brother, that had you been the man concerned, you are only for a brush, and then to yield. And truly, since this is the height of your courage now that they are at a distance from us, should they appear to you as they did to him, they might put you to second thoughts.]

But consider again, that they are only journeymen thieves; they serve under the king of the bottomless pit, who, if need be, will come to their aid himself, and his voice is as the roaring of a lion. 1 Pet 5:8. I myself have been engaged as this Little-Faith was, and I found it a terrible thing. These three villains set upon me, and as I began like a Christian to resist, they gave but a call, and in came their master. I would, as the saying is, have given my life for a penny, but that, as God would have it, I was clothed with armor of proof. Yes, and yet, though I was so harnessed, I found it hard work to quit myself like a man: no man can tell what attends us in that combat, but he who has been in the battle himself.

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Well, but they ran, you see, when they did but suppose that one Great-Grace was in the way.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: True, they have often fled, both they and their master, when Great-Grace has only appeared; and no wonder, for he is the King’s champion. But I suppose you will put some difference between Little-Faith and the King’s champion. Not all the King’s subjects are his champions; nor can they, when tried, do such feats of war as he. Is it fitting to think that a little child should handle Goliath as David did? Or that there should be the strength of an ox in a wren? Some are strong, some are weak; some have great faith, some have little: this man was one of the weak, and therefore he went to the wall.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I wish it had been Great-Grace, for their sakes.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: If it had been he, he might have had his hands full: for I must tell you, that though Great-Grace is excellently good with his weapons, and has, and can, so long as he keeps them at sword’s point, do well enough with them; yet if they get within his guard, even Faint-Heart, Mistrust, or the other, it will go hard but they will throw up his heels. And when a man is down, you know, what can he do?]

Whoever looks well upon Great-Grace’s face will see those scars and cuts there that will easily demonstrate what I say. Indeed, I once heard that he said (and that when he was in the combat), We despaired even of life. How did these sturdy rogues and their fellows make David groan, mourn, and roar! Indeed, Heman, Ps 88, and Hezekiah too, though champions in their day, were forced to bestir themselves when assaulted by these; and yet, for all that, they had their coats soundly brushed by them. Peter, upon a time, would go and try what he could do; but though some say of him that he is the prince of the apostles, they handled him so that they made him at last afraid of a mere girl.

Besides, their king is at their whistle; he is never out of hearing; and if at any time they are put to the worst, he, if possible, comes in to help them; and of him it is said, “The sword that reaches him has no effect, nor does the spear or dart or arrow. He regards iron as straw and bronze as rotten wood. No arrow can make him flee; slingstones become like chaff to him. A club is regarded as straw, and he laughs at the sound of the lance.” Job 41:26–29. What can a man do in this case? It is true, if a man could at every turn have Job’s horse, and had skill and courage to ride him, he might do notable things. “For his neck is adorned with a mane; he leaps like a locust, striking terror with his proud snorting. He paws in the valley and rejoices in his strength; he charges into battle. He laughs at fear, frightened of nothing; he does not turn back from the sword. A quiver rattles at his side, along with a flashing spear and lance. Trembling with excitement, he devours the distance; he cannot stand still when the ram’s horn sounds. At the blast of the horn, he snorts with fervor. He catches the scent of battle from afar—the shouts of captains and the cry of war.” Job 39:19–25.

But for such foot soldiers as you and I are, let us never desire to meet with an enemy, nor boast as if we could do better, when we hear of others who have been foiled, nor be tickled at the thought of our own manhood; for such commonly come off the worst when tried. Witness Peter, of whom I made mention before: he would swagger, yes, he would; he would, as his vain mind prompted him to say, do better and stand more for his Master than all men: but who was so foiled and run down by those villains as he?

When, therefore, we hear that such robberies are done on the King’s highway, there are two things it becomes us to do.

1\. To go out harnessed, and be sure to take a shield with us: for it was for lack of that, that he who laid on so vigorously at Leviathan could not make him yield; for, indeed, if that is lacking, he does not fear us at all. Therefore, he who had skill has said, “Above all, take up the shield of faith, with which you#super[+] can quench all the fiery darts of the evil one.” Eph 6:16.

2\. It is good, also, that we desire of the King a convoy, indeed, that he will go with us himself. This made David rejoice when in the Valley of the Shadow of Death; and Moses was rather for dying where he stood, than to go one step without his God. Exod 33:15.

O, my brother, if he will but go along with us, why need we be afraid of ten thousands that shall set themselves against us? Ps 3:5–8; 27:1–3. But without him, the proud helpers fall under the slain. Isa 10:4.

I, for my part, have been in the fray before now; and though (through the goodness of Him who is best) I am, as you see, alive, yet I cannot boast of any manhood. Glad shall I be if I meet with no more such brunts; though I fear we have not got beyond all danger. However, since the lion and the bear have not as yet devoured me, I hope God will also deliver us from the next uncircumcised Philistine. Then sang Christian,

#quote[“Poor Little-Faith! hast been among the thieves? \
Wast robb’d? Remember this, whoso believes, \
And get more faith; then shall you victors be \
Over ten thousand—else scarce over three.”]

So they went on, and Ignorance followed. They went then till they came to a place where they saw a way put itself into their way, and it seemed also to lie as straight as the way which they should go; and here they did not know which of the two to take, for both seemed straight before them: therefore here they stood still to consider. And as they were thinking about the way, behold, a man black of flesh, but covered with a very light robe, came to them, and asked them why they stood there. They answered that they were going to the Celestial City, but did not know which of these ways to take. “Follow me,” said the man, “it is there that I am going.” So they followed him in the way that had just come into the road, which by degrees turned, and turned them so far from the city that they desired to go to, that in a little time their faces were turned away from it; yet they followed him. But by and by, before they were aware, he led them both within the compass of a net, in which they were both so entangled that they did not know what to do; and with that the white robe fell off the black man’s back. Then they saw where they were. So there they lay crying some time, for they could not get themselves out.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then said Christian to his companion, Now I see myself in an error. Did not the shepherds tell us to beware of the Flatterer? As the saying of the wise man is, so we have found it this day: “A man who flatters his neighbor spreads a net for his feet.” Prov 29:5.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: They also gave us a note of directions about the way, so that we might find it more surely; but we have also forgotten to read it, and have not kept ourselves from the paths of the destroyer. Here David was wiser than we; for he says, “As for the deeds of men, by the word of Your lips I have kept myself from the paths of the destroyer.” Ps 17:4. Thus they lay bewailing themselves in the net. At last they spied a Shining One coming towards them with a whip of small cords in his hand. When he had come to the place where they were, he asked them where they came from, and what they were doing there. They told him that they were poor pilgrims going to Zion, but were led out of their way by a black man clothed in white, who told us, said they, to follow him, for he was going there too. Then said he with the whip, It is Flatterer, a false apostle, who has transformed himself into an angel of light. Dan 11:32; 2 Cor 11:13, 14. So he tore the net, and let the men out. Then said he to them, Follow me, that I may set you in your way again. So he led them back to the way which they had left to follow the Flatterer. Then he asked them, saying, Where did you lie last night? They said, With the shepherds upon the Delectable Mountains. He asked them then if they did not have from the shepherds a note of direction for the way. They answered, Yes. But did you not, said he, when you were at a standstill, pluck out and read your note? They answered, No. He asked them, Why? They said they forgot. He asked, moreover, if the shepherds did not tell them to beware of the Flatterer. They answered, Yes; but we did not imagine, said they, that this fine-spoken man was he. Rom 16:17, 18.]

Then I saw in my dream, that he commanded them to lie down; and when they did, he chastised them sorely, to teach them the good way in which they should walk, Deut 25:2; 2 Chr 6:27; and as he chastised them, he said, “Those I love, I rebuke and chasten. Therefore be earnest and repent.” Rev 3:19. This done, he tells them to go on their way, and take good heed to the other directions of the shepherds. So they thanked him for all his kindness, and went softly along the right way, singing,

#quote[“Come hither, you that walk along the way, \
See how the pilgrims fare that go astray: \
They catched are in an entangling net, \
Cause they good counsel lightly did forget: \
’Tis true, they rescued were; but yet, you see, \
They’re scourged to boot; let this your caution be.”]

Now, after a while, they perceived far off one coming softly, and alone, all along the highway, to meet them. Then said Christian to his companion, Over there is a man with his back towards Zion, and he is coming to meet us.

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I see him; let us take heed to ourselves now, lest he should prove a Flatterer also. So he drew nearer and nearer, and at last came up to them. His name was Atheist, and he asked them where they were going.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: We are going to Mount Zion.]

Then Atheist fell into a very great laughter.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What is the meaning of your laughter?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Atheist]: I laugh to see what ignorant persons you are, to take upon yourselves so tedious a journey, and yet you are likely to have nothing but your travel for your pains.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, man, do you think we shall not be received?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Atheist]: Received! There is no such place as you dream of in all this world.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But there is in the world to come.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Atheist]: When I was at home in my own country I heard as you now affirm, and from that hearing went out to see, and have been seeking this city these twenty years, but find no more of it than I did the first day I set out. Eccl 10:15; Jer 17:15.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: We have both heard, and believe, that there is such a place to be found.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Atheist]: Had I not believed, when at home, I would not have come this far to seek; but finding none (and yet I should have, had there been such a place to be found, for I have gone farther than you to seek it), I am going back again, and will seek to refresh myself with the things that I then cast away for hopes of that which I now see is not.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then said Christian to Hopeful his companion, Is it true, what this man has said?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Take heed, he is one of the Flatterers. Remember what it has cost us once already for listening to fellows of that kind. What! no Mount Zion? Did we not see the gate of the city from the Delectable Mountains? Also, are we not now to walk by faith? 2 Cor 5:7.]

Let us go on, lest the man with the whip overtake us again. You should have taught me the lesson that I will now sound in your ears: “Cease, my son, to hear the instruction that causes you to stray from the words of knowledge.” Prov 19:27. I say, my brother, cease to hear him, and let us believe to the saving of the soul.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: My brother, I did not put the question to you because I doubted the truth of our belief myself, but to test you, and to fetch from you a fruit of the honesty of your heart. As for this man, I know that he is blinded by the god of this world. Let you and me go on, knowing that we have belief of the truth; and no lie is of the truth. 1 John 2:21.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Now I rejoice in hope of the glory of God. So they turned away from the man; and he, laughing at them, went his way.]

I then saw in my dream that they went on until they came into a certain country whose air naturally tended to make one drowsy, if he came a stranger into it. And here Hopeful began to be very dull, and heavy with sleep; so he said to Christian, I now begin to grow so drowsy that I can scarcely hold my eyes open; let us lie down here and take one nap.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: By no means, said the other; lest, sleeping, we never wake again.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Why, my brother? Sleep is sweet to the laboring man; we may be refreshed if we take a nap.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Do you not remember that one of the shepherds told us to beware of the Enchanted Ground? He meant by that that we should beware of sleeping; so “let us not sleep as the others do, but let us remain awake and sober.” 1 Thess 5:6.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I acknowledge I was at fault; and had I been here alone, I would by sleeping have run the danger of death. I see it is true, what the wise man says, “Two are better than one.” Eccl 4:9. Until now your company has been my mercy; and you will have a good reward for your labor.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Now, then, said Christian, to prevent drowsiness in this place, let us fall into good discourse.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: With all my heart, said the other.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Where shall we begin?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Where God began with us. But you begin, if you please.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I will first sing you this song:]

#quote[“When saints do sleepy grow, let them come hither, \
And hear how these two pilgrims talk together; \
Yea, let them learn of them in any wise, \
Thus to keep open their drowsy, slumb’ring eyes. \
Saints’ fellowship, if it be managed well, \
Keeps them awake, and that in spite of hell.”]

Then Christian began, and said, I will ask you a question. How did you come to think at first of doing what you do now?

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Do you mean, how did I first come to look after the good of my soul?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, that is my meaning.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I continued a great while in the delight of those things which were seen and sold at our fair; things which, I believe now, would have drowned me in perdition and destruction, had I continued in them still.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What things were they?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: All the treasures and riches of the world. Also I delighted much in rioting, reveling, drinking, swearing, lying, uncleanness, Sabbath-breaking, and what not, that tended to destroy the soul. But I found at last, by hearing and considering things that are divine, which indeed I heard from you, as also from beloved Faithful, who was put to death for his faith and good living in Vanity Fair, that the end of these things is death, Rom 6:21–23; and that for these things’ sake the wrath of God comes upon the children of disobedience. Eph 5:6.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And did you at once fall under the power of this conviction?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: No, I was not willing at once to know the evil of sin, nor the damnation that follows upon the commission of it; but when my mind first began to be shaken with the word, I endeavored to shut my eyes against its light.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But what was the cause of your behaving like this toward the first workings of God’s blessed Spirit upon you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: The causes were, 1. I was ignorant that this was the work of God upon me. I never thought that by awakenings for sin God first begins the conversion of a sinner. 2. Sin was still very sweet to my flesh, and I was loath to leave it. 3. I could not tell how to part with my old companions, their presence and actions were so desirable to me. 4. The hours in which convictions were upon me were such troublesome and such heart-frightening hours that I could not bear, no, not so much as the remembrance of them upon my heart.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then, as it seems, at times you got rid of your trouble?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Yes, indeed, but it would come into my mind again; and then I would be as bad as I was before, indeed worse.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, what was it that brought your sins to mind again?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Many things; as,]

1\. If I so much as met a good man in the streets; or,

2\. If I heard anyone read in the Bible; or,

3\. If my head began to ache; or,

4\. If I were told that some of my neighbors were sick; or,

5\. If I heard the bell toll for some who were dead; or,

6\. If I thought of dying myself; or,

7\. If I heard that sudden death happened to others.

8\. But especially when I thought of myself, that I must quickly come to judgment.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And could you at any time, with ease, get off the guilt of sin, when by any of these ways it came upon you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: No, not I; for then they got faster hold of my conscience; and then, if I so much as thought of going back to sin (though my mind was turned against it), it would be double torment to me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what did you do then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I thought I must endeavor to mend my life; for otherwise, I thought, I am sure to be damned.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And did you endeavor to mend?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Yes, and fled not only from my sins, but from sinful company too, and gave myself to religious duties, such as praying, reading, weeping for sin, speaking truth to my neighbors, etc. These things I did, with many others, too many to relate here.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And did you think yourself well then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Yes, for a while; but at last my trouble came tumbling upon me again, and that over the neck of all my reformations.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: How did that come about, since you were now reformed?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: There were several things that brought it upon me, especially such sayings as these: “All our righteousnesses are like filthy rags.” Isa 64:6. “By the works of the law no flesh will be justified.” Gal 2:16. “When you#super[+] have done all these things, say, ‘We are unprofitable,’” Luke 17:10; with many more like them. From this I began to reason with myself like this: If all my righteousnesses are like filthy rags; if by the deeds of the law no man can be justified; and if, when we have done all, we are still unprofitable, then it is but folly to think of heaven by the law. I further thought this: If a man runs a hundred pounds into the shopkeeper’s debt, and after that pays for all that he takes; yet if his old debt still stands in the book, not crossed out, the shopkeeper may sue him for it, and cast him into prison till he pays the debt.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Well, and how did you apply this to yourself?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Why, I thought this with myself: I have by my sins run a great way into God’s book, and my reforming now will not pay off that score; therefore I should still think, under all my present amendments, But how shall I be freed from that damnation that I brought myself in danger of by my former transgressions?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: A very good application; but please go on.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Another thing that has troubled me ever since my recent amendments is that if I look closely into the best of what I do now, I still see sin, new sin, mixing itself with the best of what I do; so that now I am forced to conclude that, notwithstanding my former foolish conceits of myself and my duties, I have committed sin enough in one day to send me to hell, even if my former life had been faultless.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what did you do then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Do! I could not tell what to do, until I opened my mind to Faithful; for he and I were well acquainted. And he told me that unless I could obtain the righteousness of a man who had never sinned, neither my own righteousness nor all the righteousness of the world could save me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And did you think he spoke the truth?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Had he told me so when I was pleased and satisfied with my own amendments, I would have called him a fool for his pains; but now, since I see my own infirmity, and the sin which clings to my best performance, I have been forced to be of his opinion.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But did you think, when he first suggested it to you, that there was such a man to be found, of whom it might justly be said that he never committed sin?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I must confess the words sounded strange at first; but after a little more talk and company with him, I had full conviction about it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And did you ask him who this man was, and how you must be justified by him?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Yes, and he told me it was the Lord Jesus, who dwells at the right hand of the Most High. Heb 10:12–21. And thus, said he, you must be justified by him, even by trusting to what he has done by himself in the days of his flesh, and suffered when he hung on the tree. Rom 4:5; Col 1:14; 1 Pet 1:19. I asked him further how that man’s righteousness could be of such efficacy as to justify another before God. And he told me he was the mighty God, and did what he did, and died the death also, not for himself, but for me; to whom his doings, and the worthiness of them, would be imputed, if I believed on him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what did you do then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I made my objections against believing, because I thought he was not willing to save me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what did Faithful say to you then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: He told me to go to him and see. Then I said it was presumption. He said, No; for I was invited to come. Matt 11:28. Then he gave me a book of Jesus’ composing, to encourage me to come the more freely; and he said concerning that book that every jot and tittle of it stood firmer than heaven and earth. Matt 24:35. Then I asked him what I must do when I came; and he told me I must entreat upon my knees, Ps 95:6; Dan 6:10, with all my heart and soul, Jer 29:12, 13, the Father to reveal him to me. Then I asked him further how I must make my supplications to him; and he said, Go, and you will find him upon a mercy-seat, where he sits all the year long to give pardon and forgiveness to those who come. Exod 25:22; Lev 16:2; Num 7:89; Heb 4:16. I told him that I did not know what to say when I came; and he told me to say something to this effect: God be merciful to me a sinner, and make me know and believe in Jesus Christ; for I see that if his righteousness had not been, or if I do not have faith in that righteousness, I am utterly cast away. Lord, I have heard that you are a merciful God, and have ordained that your Son Jesus Christ should be the Savior of the world; and moreover, that you are willing to bestow him upon such a poor sinner as I am—and I am a sinner indeed. Lord, therefore take this opportunity, and magnify your grace in the salvation of my soul, through your Son Jesus Christ. Amen.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And did you do as you were told?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Yes, over, and over, and over.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And did the Father reveal the Son to you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Not at the first, nor second, nor third, nor fourth, nor fifth, no, nor at the sixth time either.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What did you do then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: What? Why, I could not tell what to do.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Did you not have thoughts of leaving off praying?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Yes; a hundred times twice over.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what was the reason you did not?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I believed that what had been told me was true, namely, that without the righteousness of this Christ all the world could not save me; and therefore, I thought to myself, if I leave off, I die, and I can but die at the throne of grace. And besides, this came into my mind: “Though it lingers, wait for it, since it will surely come and will not delay.” Hab 2:3. So I continued praying until the Father showed me his Son.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And how was he revealed to you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: I did not see him with my bodily eyes, but with the eyes of my understanding, Eph 1:18, 19; and this is how it was. One day I was very sad, I think sadder than at any one time in my life; and this sadness was through a fresh sight of the greatness and vileness of my sins. And as I was then looking for nothing but hell, and the everlasting damnation of my soul, suddenly, as I thought, I saw the Lord Jesus looking down from heaven upon me, and saying, “Believe in the Lord Jesus Christ, and you will be saved.” Acts 16:31.]

But I replied, Lord, I am a great, a very great sinner; and he answered, “My grace is sufficient for you.” 2 Cor 12:9. Then I said, But, Lord, what is believing? And then I saw from that saying, “Whoever comes to Me will never hunger, and whoever believes in Me will never thirst,” John 6:35, that believing and coming were all one; and that he who came, that is, who ran out in his heart and affections after salvation by Christ, he indeed believed in Christ. Then the water stood in my eyes, and I asked further, But, Lord, may such a great sinner as I am indeed be accepted by you, and be saved by you? And I heard him say, “And him who comes to Me I will by no means cast out.” John 6:37. Then I said, But how, Lord, must I think of you in my coming to you, that my faith may be placed rightly upon you? Then he said, “Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners.” 1 Tim 1:15. He is the end of the law for righteousness to everyone who believes. Rom 10:4, and ch. 4. He died for our sins, and rose again for our justification. Rom 4:25. He loved us, and washed us from our sins in his own blood. Rev 1:5. He is the Mediator between God and us. 1 Tim 2:5. He always lives to make intercession for us. Heb 7:25. From all of which I gathered that I must look for righteousness in his person, and for satisfaction for my sins by his blood; that what he did in obedience to his Father’s law, and in submitting to its penalty, was not for himself, but for him who will accept it for his salvation, and be thankful. And now my heart was full of joy, my eyes full of tears, and my affections running over with love to the name, people, and ways of Jesus Christ.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: This was a revelation of Christ to your soul indeed. But tell me in particular what effect this had upon your spirit.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: It made me see that all the world, notwithstanding all its righteousness, is in a state of condemnation. It made me see that God the Father, though he is just, can justly justify the coming sinner. It made me greatly ashamed of the vileness of my former life, and confounded me with the sense of my own ignorance; for never before now had a thought come into my heart that so showed me the beauty of Jesus Christ. It made me love a holy life, and long to do something for the honor and glory of the name of the Lord Jesus. Yes, I thought that if I now had a thousand gallons of blood in my body, I could spill it all for the sake of the Lord Jesus.]

I saw then in my dream that Hopeful looked back, and saw Ignorance, whom they had left behind, coming after. Look, said he to Christian, how far yonder youngster loiters behind.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, yes, I see him; he does not care for our company.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: But I believe it would not have hurt him, had he kept pace with us until now.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: That is true; but I warrant you he thinks otherwise.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: That I think he does; but, however, let us wait for him. (So they did.)]

Then Christian said to him, Come along, man; why do you stay so far behind?

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: I take my pleasure in walking alone, even a great deal more than in company, unless I like the company better.]

Then said Christian to Hopeful (but softly), Did I not tell you he did not care for our company? But, however, said he, come up, and let us talk away the time in this solitary place. Then, directing his speech to Ignorance, he said, Come, how are you? How do things stand between God and your soul now?

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: I hope, well; for I am always full of good stirrings, that come into my mind to comfort me as I walk.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What good stirrings? Please tell us.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Why, I think of God and heaven.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: So do the devils and damned souls.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: But I think of them, and desire them.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: So do many who are never likely to come there. “The soul of the sluggard desires, and has nothing.” Prov 13:4.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: But I think of them, and leave all for them.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: That I doubt; for to leave all is a very hard matter; indeed, a harder matter than many are aware of. But why, or by what, are you persuaded that you have left all for God and heaven?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: My heart tells me so.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: The wise man says, “He who trusts in his own heart is a fool.” Prov 28:26.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: That is spoken of an evil heart; but mine is a good one.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But how do you prove that?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: It comforts me in hopes of heaven.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: That may be through its deceitfulness; for a man’s heart may minister comfort to him in the hopes of that thing for which he has as yet no ground to hope.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: But my heart and life agree together; and therefore my hope is well-grounded.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Who told you that your heart and life agree together?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: My heart tells me so.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: “Ask my fellow if I am a thief.” Your heart tells you so! Unless the word of God bears witness in this matter, other testimony is of no value.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: But is it not a good heart that has good thoughts? And is not that a good life that is according to God’s commandments?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, that is a good heart that has good thoughts, and that is a good life that is according to God’s commandments; but it is one thing indeed to have these, and another thing only to think so.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Please, what do you count as good thoughts, and a life according to God’s commandments?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: There are good thoughts of various kinds; some concerning ourselves, some God, some Christ, and some other things.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: What are good thoughts concerning ourselves?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Such as agree with the word of God.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: When do our thoughts of ourselves agree with the word of God?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: When we pass the same judgment upon ourselves which the word passes. To explain myself: the word of God says of persons in a natural condition, “There is no one righteous, there is no one who does good.” It says also that “every inclination of the thoughts of man’s heart is altogether evil all the time.” Gen 6:5; Rom 3. And again, “Every inclination of man’s heart is evil from his youth.” Gen 8:21. Now, then, when we think this of ourselves, having a sense of it, then our thoughts are good ones, because they are according to the word of God.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: I will never believe that my heart is as bad as that.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Therefore you never had one good thought concerning yourself in your life. But let me go on. As the word passes a judgment upon our hearts, so it passes a judgment upon our ways; and when the thoughts of our hearts and ways agree with the judgment which the word gives of both, then both are good, because they agree with it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Explain your meaning.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, the word of God says that man’s ways are crooked ways, not good but perverse; it says they are by nature out of the good way, that they have not known it. Ps 125:5; Prov 2:15; Rom 3:12. Now, when a man thinks this of his ways, I say, when he thinks this with feeling, and with heart-humiliation, then he has good thoughts of his own ways, because his thoughts now agree with the judgment of the word of God.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: What are good thoughts concerning God?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Even, as I have said concerning ourselves, when our thoughts of God agree with what the word says of him; and that is, when we think of his being and attributes as the word has taught, of which I cannot now speak at length. But to speak of him with reference to us: we have right thoughts of God when we think that he knows us better than we know ourselves, and can see sin in us when and where we can see none in ourselves; when we think he knows our inmost thoughts, and that our heart, with all its depths, is always open to his eyes; also when we think that all our righteousness stinks in his nostrils, and that therefore he cannot bear to see us stand before him in any confidence, even in all our best performances.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Do you think that I am such a fool as to think that God can see no further than I can; or that I would come to God in the best of my performances?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, what do you think in this matter?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: Why, to be short, I think I must believe in Christ for justification.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What! You think you must believe in Christ, when you do not see your need of him! You see neither your original nor your actual infirmities; but have such an opinion of yourself, and of what you do, as plainly shows you to be one who never saw the necessity of Christ’s personal righteousness to justify you before God. How, then, do you say, I believe in Christ?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: I believe well enough, for all that.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: How do you believe?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: I believe that Christ died for sinners; and that I shall be justified before God from the curse, through his gracious acceptance of my obedience to his laws. Or thus: Christ makes my religious duties acceptable to his Father by virtue of his merits, and so I shall be justified.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Let me give an answer to this confession of your faith.]

1\. You believe with a fantastical faith; for this faith is nowhere described in the word.

2\. You believe with a false faith; because it takes justification from the personal righteousness of Christ, and applies it to your own.

3\. This faith does not make Christ a justifier of your person, but of your actions; and of your person for your actions’ sake, which is false.

4\. Therefore this faith is deceitful, even such as will leave you under wrath in the day of God Almighty: for true justifying faith puts the soul, aware of its lost condition by the law, upon fleeing for refuge to Christ’s righteousness (which righteousness of his is not an act of grace by which he makes your obedience accepted with God for justification, but his personal obedience to the law, in doing and suffering for us what the law required at our hands); this righteousness, I say, true faith accepts; and the soul, being shrouded under its skirt, and by it presented as spotless before God, is accepted, and acquitted from condemnation.

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: What! Would you have us trust to what Christ in his own person has done without us? This notion would loosen the reins of our lust, and allow us to live as we please; for what does it matter how we live, if we may be justified from all by Christ’s personal righteousness, when we believe it?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Ignorance is your name, and as your name is, so are you: even this answer of yours demonstrates what I say. Ignorant you are of what justifying righteousness is, and as ignorant how to secure your soul, through faith in it, from the heavy wrath of God. Yes, you are also ignorant of the true effects of saving faith in this righteousness of Christ, which is to bow and win over the heart to God in Christ, to love his name, his word, ways, and people, and not as you ignorantly imagine.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Ask him if he ever had Christ revealed to him from heaven.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: What! You are a man for revelations! I do believe that what both you and all the rest of you say about that matter is but the fruit of distracted brains.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Hopeful]: Why, man, Christ is so hidden in God from the natural understanding of the flesh that he cannot be savingly known by any man, unless God the Father reveals him to him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: That is your faith, but not mine; yet mine, I do not doubt, is as good as yours, though I do not have so many whimsies in my head as you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Give me leave to put in a word. You ought not to speak so lightly of this matter: for this I will boldly affirm, even as my good companion has done, that no man can know Jesus Christ but by the revelation of the Father; yes, and faith too, by which the soul lays hold upon Christ (if it is right), must be wrought by the exceeding greatness of his mighty power, Matt 11:27; 1 Cor 12:3; Eph 1:17–19; the working of which faith, I perceive, poor Ignorance, you are ignorant of. Be awakened, then, see your own wretchedness, and flee to the Lord Jesus; and by his righteousness, which is the righteousness of God (for he himself is God), you will be delivered from condemnation.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ignorance]: You go so fast I cannot keep pace with you; you go on ahead: I must stay a while behind.]

Then they said,

#quote[“Well, Ignorance, wilt thou yet foolish be, \
To slight good counsel, ten times given thee? \
And if thou yet refuse it, thou shalt know, \
Ere long, the evil of thy doing so. \
Remember, man, in time: stoop, do not fear: \
Good counsel, taken well, saves; therefore hear. \
But if thou yet shalt slight it, thou wilt be \
The loser, Ignorance, I’ll warrant thee.”]
